; ESC means "back to the attract
; screen", which is what quitting a game
; means here; only running out of tanks
; is worth a card.
to show.game.over
  local "r
  local "who
  textscreen
  ct
  setcursor [15 10]
  print [GAME OVER]
  setcursor [11 12]
  (print [FINAL SCORE:] :score)
  setcursor [11 14]
  (print [TANKS KILLED:] :kills)
  make "r score.rank :score
  if 0 = :r [wait 2000 stop]
  setcursor [12 18]
  print "|A NEW HIGH SCORE|
  setcursor [5 20]
  print "|Type a name, then Enter|
  flush.keys
  make "who read.name
  insert.score :r :score :who
  save.scores
end

;======================================
; One game
;
; `cs` restores automatic refresh, so it
; comes BEFORE the `setrefresh "sync`
; and not after -- Asteroids logged that
; one the hard way. `window` comes after
; `cs` for the same reason: `cs` puts
; the boundary mode back to `wrap`, and
; a wireframe drawn under `wrap` smears
; a horizon segment across the whole
; screen and looks exactly like a
; projection fault.
;
; The clock is NOT asked for here. It is
; asked for once per session, in
; `battlezone`, because it costs a bus
; teardown on a board with a radio up
; and a player starting their fourth
; game should not pay for it again.
to init.game
  cs
  splitscreen
  ct
  window
  ht
  make "px 800
  make "pz 800
  make "ph 0
  cam.setup
  cam.offsets
  ob.scan
  make "sh.on false
  make "sh.cool 0
  make "es.on false
  make "bm.n 0
  make "tk.boom 0
  make "e.boom 0
  make "cracked false
  make "kills 0
  make "hits 0
  make "rd.sw 0
  make "score 0
  make "lives :start.lives
  make "extra.due :extra.at
  make "e.score 0
  make "e.diff 0
  make "e.rage 255
  make "e.tmr 640
  make "ms.n 0
  make "e.gun false
  make "e.ram false
  make "e.drift false
  make "au.in 0
  spawn.enemy
  make "paused false
  make "quit false
  make "playing true
  make "frame.count 0
  make "hud.n 0
  make "hud.bs 0
  make "hud.fs 0
  make "hud.bm 0
  make "hud.fm 0
  draw.score
  (setrefresh "sync :fps)
  ; A baseline poll, so the keypress
  ; that started the game is not
  ; delivered to the first frame as a
  ; hit.
  pollkeys
end

; The state machine is two flags.
; `playing` ends a game -- ESC or the
; last tank -- and `leaving` ends the
; session, which only the attract screen
; sets.
;
; `stopsound` is here rather than in
; `battlezone` because the engine idles
; on a re-gate every frame and the frame
; loop has just stopped: without this
; the last note it queued plays over the
; game-over card.
to one.game
  attract.screen
  if :leaving [stop]
  init.game
  until [not :playing] [play.frame]
  setrefresh "auto
  stopsound
  if not :quit [show.game.over]
end

;======================================
; The session
;
; ASK FOR THE CLOCK ONCE AND GIVE IT
; BACK ONCE. M2 did both inside the
; game, which was right when there was
; one game a session; now that ESC
; returns to an attract screen, doing it
; per game would tear the wireless bus
; down and rebuild it every time a
; player pressed a key. The clock is a
; property of the session.
;
; AND THERE IS A WAY OUT. M2's entry
; point could only be left by quitting
; the game, and this one could not be
; left at all if the attract screen took
; only Space -- which would strand the
; board at 300 MHz for the rest of the
; session, and that is exactly the thing
; `restore.clock` exists to prevent. ESC
; on the attract screen is the door, and
; it is the reason `leaving` is a flag
; rather than a `forever`.
;
; THE SAME FLAG CARRIES THE REFUSAL, and
; that is the whole of what a board
; without the clock costs this file: a
; session that begins already `leaving`
; runs no game and falls straight
; through to the tail, where the only
; difference is which of two things gets
; printed. `stopsound`, `setrefresh` and
; `restore.clock` are no-ops on that
; path because nothing was started and
; the clock was never changed.
;
; ONE EXIT, and the reason it has to be
; one: the tail does `textscreen ct`,
; so anything printed before it is
; wiped. The refusal has to come after
; the clear rather than instead of the
; game, which is also why `no.clock`
; does not clear the screen itself.
to battlezone
  setup.sound
  make "leaving not clock
  until [:leaving] [one.game]
  stopsound
  setrefresh "auto
  restore.clock
  textscreen
  ct
  settc [7 0]
  ifelse "fast = :cpu.at [
    print [Battlezone M5]
  ] [
    no.clock
  ]
end
